  "id": "carranga_boyacense",
  "name": "Carranga (Boyacá campesino string music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Carranga is the rural string-band song of the Boyacá and Cundinamarca highlands, shaped from 1977 by Jorge Velosa and Los Carrangueros de Ráquira out of the region's campesino merengue, rumba and paseo dance tunes. It is played on tiple, requinto (a small high-pitched tiple), guitar and guacharaca scraper, with humorous, plain-spoken verses about peasant life. The requinto card stands in for the Colombian requinto and the guayo card for the guacharaca.",
  "recipe": "classic South American late-1970s carranga, ballad Poetry, Latin am troubadour branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, unprocessed natural acoustic. tiple, credited requinto guayo source unspecified, classical nylon-string guitar. live tracking. shure SM57, transformer-coupled solid-state pre, colored vintage console, optical compressor. tape 15ips spring reverb.",
